00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:14.340
Question number six, how
does Google secure my data?

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.460
CORI PEELE: We encrypt
at rest and in transit.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.390
Our infrastructure doesn't
rely on any single technology

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:22.380
to make it secure.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.900
We've designed security
of our infrastructure

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.950
in layers that builds upon
each other from the physical

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.950
security of the data
centers to these security

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:34.900
protections in our
hardware and software

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.910
to the processes we use to
support operational security.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.880
This layered protection
creates strong security

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.520
as a foundation for
everything we do.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.230
Data stored on
our infrastructure

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:50.850
is automatically encrypted
at rest by default

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.180
and distributed for
availability and reliability.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.260
We also support
customer-managed encryption keys

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:02.520
using Cloud QMS and
customer-supplied encryption

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:03.450
keys.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.360
Communication over the
internet to our cloud services

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.920
are encrypted.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:09.840
The scale of our
infrastructure allows

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.350
us to absorb many denial
of service attacks,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.740
and we have multiple layers of
protection that further reduce

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.390
the risk of any DDoS impact.

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:23.220
Finally, our operations team
detects threats and responds

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.664
to incidents around the clock.

00:01:25.664 --> 00:01:27.330
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Security is important,

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.070
and applications are
often business-critical

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.986
to our customers, so
they need to be confident

00:01:30.986 --> 00:01:33.330
that Google Cloud is
reliable and scalable.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.830
Question number
seven-- is Google

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.020
ready for the enterprise?

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:36.795
YURI GRINSHTEYN: That
is a great question

00:01:36.795 --> 00:01:38.780
that every customer
should be asking,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.160
and we got lots of customers
to answer that one for us.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.410
You can read what they have
to say about this by going

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.446
to cloud.google.com/customers.

00:01:45.446 --> 00:01:47.820
You'll see that folks from
Charles Schwab and the Chicago

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.790
Department of Transportation
to Johnson and Johnson

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.530
and the National
Institute on Aging

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.360
have all decided that Google
Cloud is reliable, scalable,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.169
and secure enough for them.

00:01:57.169 --> 00:01:58.210
Analysts agree with them.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.480
For example, Forrester
just named Google Cloud

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:03.300
a leader in their Public
Cloud Platform Native Security

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.110
Report.

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.510
This all just goes to show
our combination of world class

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.789
infrastructure, research
and thought leadership,

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:11.910
and folks just like these
customer engineers next to me

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:14.280
all add up to making
Google Cloud the place

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.320
to run enterprise workloads.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.120
CORI PEELE: That sure
makes us feel better

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:21.270
about running business-critical
applications on GCP.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.340
But on to question eight--

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.692
how do I control my cloud costs?

00:02:25.692 --> 00:02:27.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
We have many ways

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:29.171
to help you estimate
and control your costs.

00:02:29.171 --> 00:02:31.170
You're always able to use
the pricing calculator

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.336
to help estimate what your
workload costs might look

00:02:33.336 --> 00:02:34.710
like once you're on the cloud.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.001
Once you're up and running,
your instances and services

00:02:37.001 --> 00:02:39.467
can be labeled to help
organize your resources.

00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:41.550
Building data can be
exported to BigQuery in order

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.647
to review and analyze what
you're spending in case

00:02:43.647 --> 00:02:45.480
the default bills aren't
descriptive enough.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.340
Plus, you can set up
budgets and alerting

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.590
from your cloud console to
warn you well ahead of time

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.249
before you spend your budget.

00:02:51.249 --> 00:02:53.040
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Oh,
I dropped my contacts.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.164
CORI PEELE: Oh, we'll help.

00:02:54.164 --> 00:02:57.090
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Oh, we'll help you.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.320
NICK ORLOVE: And we're back.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:58.980
Let's see.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.120
Where were we?

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:02.040
That's right--
question number nine.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.620
What is a managed service, and
what problems does it solve?

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.161
IVAN RAMIREZ: Well,
a managed service

00:03:06.161 --> 00:03:07.824
is exactly what it
says on the box.

00:03:07.824 --> 00:03:09.240
It's a set of
services or products

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.030
where Google takes care of
the upkeep and maintenance.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.160
Since its inception, Google
has put a strong focus

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.660
on marrying open
source technologies

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.220
with fully managed services.

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:18.780
While we focus on
the maintenance,

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.905
you get the product
that truly stands out,

00:03:20.905 --> 00:03:22.530
allowing you to
refocus your engineer's

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.720
time from a
maintenance-centric approach

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.046
to a feature-focused strategy
that ensures your time

00:03:27.046 --> 00:03:28.920
and effort are being
spent on the things that

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.800
will set your application and
company apart from the rest.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.900
A good example of
this no-ops approach

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.075
is best shown when we
discuss the concept

00:03:36.075 --> 00:03:37.590
of [? flat ?] migrations.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.840
With Google Cloud, we
seamlessly migrate your instance

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.173
to another machine through
Google's hardware maintenance

00:03:42.173 --> 00:03:45.180
events so that you don't
suffer any downtime.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:47.160
Most of our users
don't even notice.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.190
NICK ORLOVE: Finally--
drumroll, please--

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.510
question number 10.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.250
What makes GCP special?

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.870
STEPHANIE WONG:
Well, us, of course.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:03:56.730
And in addition,
Google Cloud Platform

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:59.590
differs in a variety of ways,
including our security model,

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:01.200
which is an end-to-end
process built

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.960
over 15 years of experience
keeping our customers

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.300
safe on Google apps like Gmail.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:07.710
We offer a global network.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:09.330
Our network backbone
has thousands

00:04:09.330 --> 00:04:11.820
of miles of
fiberoptic cable using

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.920
advanced software-defined
networking and Edge

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.649
caches globally to deliver
fast, consistent performance.

00:04:17.649 --> 00:04:19.890
We also live and
breathe big data.

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:23.280
We contributed to innovations
like MapReduce, Bigtable,

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.800
and Dremel, and new frameworks
for data warehousing

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.630
and machine learning, even batch
and real time data processing.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.462
Our solutions are
serverless, and they

00:04:31.462 --> 00:04:33.420
let you take advantage
of the years of research

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:34.710
that we've done.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:37.327
And we offer Compute Engine
billing by the second,

00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:39.160
so you don't have to
pay for compute minutes

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.680
that you don't use.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.990
And of course, we are
environmentally friendly,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.050
and our data centers run on half
of the energy of a typical data

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:46.680
center.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.640
In 2017, we purchased
100% renewable energy

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:53.280
to match our consumption
for our global operations.

